850-118!Rob.Adams@sawasdi.apana.org.au (Rob Adams) writes:
>[ERR]:
>> The responsibility not to publicly distribute the expressions, images
>> or particulars of children without the consent of their parents.

As mentioned earlier, there is a problem with "the consent of their
parents" in that responsibilities can't be guaranteed by law. It's
difficult to see why abuses of this responsibility shouldn't be dealt
with by the law. This one might, perhaps, be better framed as a right.
Something like,

  The right of every adult to control and license public distribution of 
  their own expressions, images and particulars, and the right of parents to 
  control and license public distribution of the expressions, images and 
  particulars of their children.

Note that this might lend support to (some) legal remedies to kiddie porn.
          
>I would like this one to read "without the consent of the child and it's
>parent(s)".

"My daughter has run away from home. She's blonde, fourteen, wearing 
 bell-bottom pants and a fluffy black wig ..."

Basically, children are not thought to have the ability to consent to some
usages. They are necessarily naive, and need the intervention of their parents.
Of course there are cases where this is not the most sensible way to have
things; some parents are not fit to be parents. However it is difficult to
see how to frame a right that covers such cases.

>> The responsibility of the authors of freely-redistributable information
>> to make it publicly available in electronic form.
> 
>....limited only by economics.

I now think that this whole second responsibility, while desirable, is
not essential to the ERR. I'd like to keep non-essentials out of the
document, so that it stays focused. If you can think of why this
responsibility is essential to the document, please correct me.

