I am also replying to your earlier messages, where you are drawing a 
distinction between telephone dialups, and leased lines. I have not found 
a definition of "telecommunications device", and it is not contained in 
the amendment itself, but this bill certainly applies to anyone using a 
telephone, and to anyone who PROVIDES communications services to 
telephone users. It therefore does affect a large segment of the Net, 
even if your distinction is correct.

I will look for a definition of "telecommunications device" but I am 
reasonably comfortable that it applies to all computer connections.

As to your question, the difference in the mediums that I was referring 
to was, in part, what you referred to earlier. We are NOT engaged in 
telephone conversations here, which are one on one. Here, the instant I 
hit the enter key, my message goes to thousands of people, each of whom 
have a choice to read it, or pass on it. Not entirely true with a 
telephone call.

Plus, your computer doesn't ring in the middle of the night when I send 
you a message. <g>. There is also something more "remote" about this 
medium. While we can all connect with each other in seconds, a "threat" 
in echomail is USUALLY not quite as serious as a threat by voice phone. 

OTOH, this medium is more permanent than the telephone. Absent a tape 
recorder, voice communications are fleeting - they "disappear" as soon as 
the words are spoken or heard. Here, copies of the communications are 
made thousands of times, to thousands of people, with potential for more 
damage than the telephone. But the Exon amendment is not about preventing 
damage, it is about controlling free speech. <g>.



