>what is the distinguishing difference in the mediums that makes the 
>extension a problem? <g>.

Interactive media is a hybrid of every existing media of communication today,
but at the same time it is unique in several ways that make it so drastically
different from those media that no intelligent person would seek to simply
extend existing regulation to it...

1) Interactive media is not like broadcast media (television, radio, etc.)
   While you have SOME sort of information control over your television
   (i.e. you can change the channel, turn the volume up or down, etc.) the
   content is largely out of your control.  In contrast, nearly EVERY SINGLE
   PIECE of information you view on interactive systems (such as BB systems,
   the internet, and interactive cable television systems) is selected and
   viewed VOLUNTARILY.  That's right, if you want porn, you have to go find
   it, usually decode it, and view it, all in the course of three or four
   individual concious choices.  The image of people being forcibly exposed
   to or assualted by obscenity on the Internet is an outright myth, and those
   who use the Internet (unlike the know-nothing bureaucrats that wrote S 314)
   realize that to be true.

   Harassing and unsolicited obscene messages are an entirely different
   story, and I'm aware of very few people who are opposed to the sections of
   the legislation that criminalize sending these through telecommunications
   media.

   Moreover, it is technically possible for people to personally "censor"
   newsgroups, hosts, web sites, and other areas of the Net they don't want
   to see or want their kids to see.

2) Interactive media isn't even like a telephone system...it has more uses,
   is more flexible, and it is (usually) easier to trace the originators of
   harassing/threatening messages over computerized networks than it is through
   the phone system.

Children are not going to be forcibly exposed to porn on the Internet as some
have suggested: child molesters are not roaming the bitstream and sending
dirty pictures to little kiddies.  Even if they were, the sections of the law
pertaining to harassing or unsolicited obscene messages would take care of the
problem. But no one ever just "trips" over anything on the Internet...granted
the Playboy and Penthouse WWW pages might be in a grey area, and maybe
regulation is needed to curb out and out surprise displays of porn like that
(though I'm personally not too thrilled about the idea).

If a little kid wants porn, he has to go out and find it.  If it's on the
Usenet he/she has to intentionally decode the file, and intentionally view it.
What kind of kids are on the Internet anyway? They're either in school or in a
middle to upper class home with parents who have to take some responsibility
for buying their kid a computer and a modem.

Little little kids have no interest in seeing naked people roll around with
each other...older little kids might, and older older kids definitely do. But
I think it comes down to parenting and teaching your kid wrong from right.
Your kid might be killed by shoving a fork into an electrical outlet, but no
one wants to ban electrical outlets in houses do they? No, they teach their
kids that shoving forks into outlets is a BAD THING.  So you don't want your
kid to read porn? Tell him/her that it's a BAD THING. Before you let your
kid loose on the Net, or before teachers let other people's kids loose out
here, I think a little education is in order.  Tell the kids not to believe
everything they see and hear, that not everyone on the Net is nice and honest,
that, yes, like in real life, there's some bad shit in here...but the Net is
not a Children's Reading Room or a preschool playground, it's a massive
communications media that you can control almost completely on your own.  You
don't have to read Usenet, you don't have to connect to bulletin-boards, you
don't have to FTP to certain places.  You do, read, and see what you want to.

Are kids going to do bad things? Yes, but it's still not my job to make sure
your kid doesn't do them, especially when it affects no one but the child
himself. Some people (my girlfriend included) might argue that it is, in fact,
my job to take care of other people's kids, but that's a matter of opinion...

Lost my point somewhere,
Mick

