I apologize for this slightly off-topic post, but I would like to relate the 
following quote to you.  Dose anyone find this as bothersome as I do?

Madison County, Idaho,Sheriff Greg Moffat, (President of a sheriffs 
association) said Friday that the sheriffs (of the association) opposed the 
U.S. Militia Association "because of the very sort of thing you see in the 
news (the Oklahoma City bombing).  We're concerned about the type of people 
they attract and some of their radical ideas...."

When an elected government official (a sheriff or group of sheriffs), opposes 
an organization because of their ideas; I begin to worry about where our 
government is headed.  The existence of this organization is protected by the 
first amendment, as are the "ideas" that this organization has.  Of course, 
Sheriff Moffat's ideas are also protected, up to the point he infringes on 
other people's ideas.

The bombing in Oklahoma is sad.  It is scary that someone would do this; but 
it will be even more scary if the government uses this to squash the Bill of 
Rights.  We haven't closed down the US Postal Service - even though numerous 
postal employees have gone on rampages at work and killed people - so why 
should we close down all militias, just because a small group of people who 
may or may not have been involved with one militia decided to bomb a 
government office building?  

As I write this (the Saturday after the bombing), the government is making 
many claims about the individuals involved with the bombing, and about the 
individuals involved with these so-called militias in general.  They are 
mixing truth with fiction, and this worries me.  I am not a member of any of 
these organizations - to me they seem somewhat outgunned - but I have no doubt 
that they will defend the constitution of our country if the government ever 
tries to take away our constitutional rights, (something the various state 
national guard units may or may not do).

And sheriff Moffat is "concerned" about their "radical ideas" ???

Well, that's my two point three cents worth!

Sean Sengenberger
Boise Idaho


